module return_arm.pos_1

// A BARE `return` AS THE BODY OF A MATCH ARM: `Kind.A => return 1`, without
// the braces that made the same thing readable since wave B9.
//
// WHY THIS FORM, measured 2026-09-20. Of the four arm shapes the parser stopped
// at, this one opened the most files -- five against one each for a string- and
// a char-literal arm. The count is not of occurrences but of files whose FIRST
// refusal is this shape, because later spans in a file are recovery debris.
//
// THE BRACED TWIN IS HERE ON PURPOSE. `A => return 1` and `A => { return 1 }`
// are the same fact with different punctuation, and every judge that tells them
// apart is a judge that will drift. Both spellings in one program, both
// reaching the same answer, is what makes the FORM the subject rather than its
// acceptance.
//
// AND THE MIXED ARM, which is the one that found the real hole. A match whose
// arms all return yields the enclosing function no value; a match where ONE arm
// returns and another produces a value still has a type, and it comes from the
// arm that produces one. The value-position match is the only shape that asks
// the lattice about an arm body at all -- a statement-position match never did,
// which is why the ice needed a tail to reproduce.

type Kind enum A | B

fn all_return(k Kind) -> int {
    match k {
        Kind.A => return 1
        Kind.B => return 2
    }
}

fn braced(k Kind) -> int {
    match k {
        Kind.A => { return 1 }
        Kind.B => { return 2 }
    }
}

fn mixed(k Kind, n int) -> int {
    match k {
        A => return n
        B => 7
    }
}

fn main() {
    println("${all_return(Kind.A)}")
    println("${braced(Kind.B)}")
    println("${mixed(Kind.B, 3)}")
    println("${all_return(Kind.B) == braced(Kind.B)}")
}
